Theory
GitHub is a cloud-based version control platform that allows developers to store, manage, and collaborate on source code. Jenkins can be integrated with GitHub to automate the software development process.
Continuous Integration (CI) is the practice of frequently merging code changes into a shared repository. Each change is automatically built and tested to identify errors early.
Continuous Delivery or Deployment (CD) automates the process of preparing or releasing software after successful testing.

The integration process involves the following components:
- GitHub Repository: Stores source code and tracks changes.
- Webhook: Notifies Jenkins when code changes occur.
- Jenkins Pipeline: Automates building and testing.
- Jenkinsfile: Defines the pipeline stages as code.
This integration improves collaboration, reduces integration errors, and ensures faster software delivery.


Procedure:

Create a GitHub repository and upload the software project.
Configure GitHub credentials and the repository URL in Jenkins.
Create a Jenkins Pipeline job and configure the source code repository.
Create a Jenkinsfile containing build and testing stages.
Configure a GitHub webhook to trigger Jenkins automatically on code changes.
Execute the pipeline and verify the build and test results.
